- [x] Record the VR4300 scheduling facts used by the compiler work.
  - Local reference: `docs/VR4300-Users-Manual.pdf`.  The local
    `docs/JZ4780_pm.pdf` copy is a peripheral module manual and was not used
    for CPU pipeline timing.
  - Branch and jump instructions have one architectural delay slot.
  - Integer load-use hazards normally cost one pipeline cycle when the next
    instruction consumes the loaded value; the hardware interlock makes this a
    performance hazard, not a correctness reason to keep blanket `nop`s.
  - Stores can keep the data cache busy for a following data-cache access; the
    VR4300 documents this as a store delay slot with hardware interlocks.  A
    trailing store `nop` is therefore only useful when it would otherwise hide a
    real following memory access stall.
  - FP compare/computational and `LWC1`/`LDC1` paths have FP bypass limits, so
    FP load/compute latency must be handled by scheduling rather than blanket
    deletion.
  - Integer `mult`/`multu` are about 5 cycles; `div`/`divu` are about
    37 cycles, so strength reduction is high-value on this CPU.
  - Double FP add/sub are about 3 cycles, double multiply about 8 cycles, and
    double divide about 58 cycles; dependent FP use also needs spacing.
  - VR4300 I-cache is 16 KB and D-cache is 8 KB, so code size and spill
    traffic matter on real N64 hardware more than they appear to on Malta.

